.TH SKIPPER 1 "August 2026" "Skipper 360 v0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
skipper \- Automatic password keeper and PTY session supervisor CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B skipper
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Skipper 360
is an automatic password keeper and terminal pseudo-terminal (PTY) session supervisor for Linux systems.
It monitors pseudo-terminals in real-time, detects interactive password prompts using regex pattern matching, and securely retrieves and injects credentials stored in your operating system's Keyring (GNOME Keyring, KDE KWallet, or Freedesktop Secret Service API).

All sensitive memory operations are hardened using CPU speculation barriers (\fBLFENCE\fR / \fBISB\fR), cache line alignment (64-byte boundaries), physical cache line flushing (\fBCLFLUSH\fR), and memory zeroing (\fBzeroize\fR).

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B init
Initialize Skipper 360 with default user credentials and master password.
Pre-fills your current OS environment username and securely stores your master password in the OS Keyring under key \fBdefault_password\fR. Creates the default configuration file at \fB~/.config/skipper360/config.toml\fR.

.TP
.B activate
Activate active prompt monitoring.
Ensures the background daemon (\fBskipperd\fR) is running (auto-spawns it if inactive) and sends an IPC activation request.

.TP
.B deactivate
Deactivate prompt monitoring without shutting down the daemon.

.TP
.B status
Display current system operational status, active daemon process PID, active PTY session count, timeout configuration, and a table of all registered whitelist entries with their match modes and expiration countdowns.

.TP
.B mode \fISTANDARD|SILENT\fR
Configure operational mode:
.RS
.IP \fBStandard\fR
Display interactive console messages when prompts are detected and passwords are injected.
.IP \fBSilent\fR
Perform prompt detection and credential injection silently without printing status messages.
.RE

.TP
.B whitelist \fR[\fIaction\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Manage command whitelist entries and custom command-specific credentials.
Subcommands:
.RS
.IP "\fBadd\fR [\fI--mode exact|prefix\fR] [\fI--ttl DURATION\fR] \fICOMMAND...\fR"
Add a command to the whitelist. Prompts interactively for a command-specific password (leave empty to use default master password).
.RS
.IP "\fB-m, --mode\fR \fIexact|prefix\fR"
Matching strategy: \fBprefix\fR (default) matches commands starting with the string; \fBexact\fR requires identical match.
.IP "\fB-t, --ttl\fR \fIDURATION\fR"
Set a temporary expiration duration (e.g. \fB30s\fR, \fB10m\fR, \fB1h\fR, \fB2d\fR, or seconds \fB3600\fR). Expired entries are automatically purged.
.RE
.IP "\fBdelete\fR \fICOMMAND...\fR"
Remove a command from the whitelist and delete its credential from the OS Keyring.
.IP "\fBlist\fR [\fI--show\fR]"
Display a table of registered whitelist entries. If \fB--show\fR is specified, displays cleartext passwords (requires \fBsudo\fR / root privileges).
.RE

.TP
.B run \fICOMMAND...\fR
Execute a command under Skipper 360 PTY supervision (e.g. \fBskipper run sudo pacman -Syu\fR).
Streams stdout and stderr output in real-time over Unix Domain Socket IPC, monitors PTY output for password prompts, and performs zeroed memory injection.

.TP
.B audit
Display the security audit log history in a formatted UTF-8 table. Shows timestamps, user, process PID, actions (\fBPasswordInjected\fR, \fBPromptDetected\fR, \fBWhitelistAdded\fR, \fBWhitelistDeleted\fR), command slugs, and details. Reads from \fB~/.config/skipper360/audit.log\fR (POSIX mode 0600).

.TP
.B log \fR[\fIshow|clear\fR]
Manage daemon execution logs.
.RS
.IP "\fBshow\fR [\fI--lines N\fR]"
Display the last \fIN\fR lines (default: 50) of the current daily rolling daemon log file.
.IP "\fBclear\fR"
Purge all log files in \fB~/.config/skipper360/logs/\fR.
.RE

.TP
.B completion \fIBASH|ZSH|FISH|POWERSHELL|ELVISH\fR
Generate shell auto-completion script for the specified shell.

.TP
.B doctor
Run a comprehensive diagnostic health check verifying configuration file integrity, OS Keyring backend availability, IPC socket state, and POSIX PTY platform compatibility.

.TP
.B reset
Reset all Skipper 360 configuration files and wipe stored keyring credentials.

.SH ENVIRONMENT

.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Specifies the base directory for user configuration. Defaults to \fB~/.config\fR. Skipper files are stored in \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/skipper360/\fR.
.TP
.B USER
Default username pre-filled during \fBskipper init\fR.
.TP
.B PATH
Used by \fBskipper-cli\fR to locate and auto-spawn the \fBskipperd\fR daemon executable.

.SH FILES

.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/config.toml
Main TOML configuration file containing general settings, default credentials, whitelist entries, and custom prompt patterns. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/skipper.sock
Unix Domain Socket used for client-daemon IPC communication. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/skipper.pid
PID lockfile ensuring single-instance daemon operation.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/audit.log
Append-only JSON-lines security audit event log. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/logs/skipper.log.YYYY-MM-DD
Daily rolling daemon execution log files. Mode 0600, directory mode 0700.

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error (configuration, IPC, Keyring, or command failure).
.TP
.B 101
Fatal Rust panic or unhandled internal error.
.TP
.B 127
Command not found or daemon binary missing from PATH.

.SH EXAMPLES

.TP
.B Initialize Skipper 360:
$ skipper init
.TP
.B Activate background monitoring:
$ skipper activate
.TP
.B Add a command with 1-hour expiration TTL:
$ skipper whitelist add "ssh user@staging" --ttl 1h
.TP
.B Execute a command under PTY supervision:
$ skipper run sudo pacman -Syu
.TP
.B View security audit history:
$ skipper audit
.TP
.B View recent daemon execution logs:
$ skipper log show --lines 100

